{
  "title": "Design Financing Architecture for East Africa Rural Electrification Scale-Up",
  "content": "This plan designs a comprehensive blended finance architecture to scale distributed solar + storage from 12K to 500K users across East Africa with $200-250M over 5 years. The work decomposes into parallel research streams covering (1) benchmark financing structures from comparable energy access projects, (2) specific donor/DFI/investor mapping, (3) financial instrument design, and (4) capital needs modeling, followed by a synthesis step that integrates everything into a cohesive financing architecture document.\n\nUncertainty is moderate—the sector (off-grid energy in East Africa) is well-established with known players, but instrument structuring and sequencing require careful design. Decomposability is high—research streams are independent. Therefore join='all' with parallel research feeding into synthesis.",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "goal": "Research 5-8 comparable off-grid/mini-grid energy access projects in Sub-Saharan Africa that successfully scaled to 200K+ users, focusing on their financing structures, capital stacks, blending ratios (grant/concessional/commercial), key financing milestones, and lessons learned. Include projects like M-KOPA, d.light, Engie PowerCorner, PowerGen, SunCulture, and any relevant GCF/IDA-funded programs. Extract specific data on how they layered grants, concessional debt, guarantees, and equity at different growth stages.",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "goal": "Build a comprehensive, prioritized donor/investor target list for off-grid solar + storage in East Africa (Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda, with expansion to Ghana/Senegal). Research and categorize by: (1) Grant funders (bilateral: USAID/Power Africa, DFID/FCDO, GIZ, Sida, NORAD; multilateral: GCF, GEF, AfDB SEFA, World Bank ESMAP, EU Africa Infrastructure Trust Fund), (2) Concessional lenders (IFC, FMO, Proparco, CDC/BII, OPIC/DFC, EIB, AfDB), (3) Guarantee providers (MIGA, GuarantCo, USAID DCA, InfraCredit), (4) Impact investors (Acumen, SunFunder/now Nuveen, responsAbility, Triodos, ARCH Emerging Markets Partners, CrossBoundary Energy Access), (5) Outcomes-based funders (DFID/FCDO results-based financing, EnDev RBF, World Bank OGEF). For each, include: typical ticket size, instrument type, geographic focus, current energy access portfolio, application timeline, and strategic fit score (high/medium/low).",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "goal": "Design the detailed blended finance instrument architecture for the $200-250M capital stack over 5 years. Structure the following layers with rough allocation percentages and dollar amounts: (1) First-loss/catalytic capital layer: grants + technical assistance ($X), (2) Concessional debt layer: below-market loans from DFIs ($X), (3) Risk mitigation layer: guarantees and first-loss facilities ($X), (4) Commercial debt layer: local currency and hard currency facilities ($X), (5) Equity/quasi-equity layer: impact investors + internal equity ($X), (6) Results-based financing layer: outcome payments tied to connections/productive use ($X). For each layer, specify: purpose (capex, opex, working capital, end-user financing), tenor, pricing, security/collateral, currency, disbursement triggers, and how it de-risks the layer above it. Model the phasing across Year 1-2 ($40-50M) vs Year 3-5 ramp-up. Account for the existing $8M grant funding. Target blended return of 4-6%.",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": [0]
    },
    {
      "goal": "Develop a rough order-of-magnitude capital needs model for scaling from 12,000 to 500,000 users across Kenya, Tanzania, and Uganda over 5 years. Break down costs into: (1) Hardware capex per connection (solar home systems vs mini-grids, storage, productive use appliances—irrigation pumps, cold storage, phone charging), (2) Soft costs (installation, customer acquisition, training), (3) End-user financing/receivables (PAYGo working capital needs), (4) Operating costs (maintenance, customer service, platform/technology), (5) Country expansion costs (licensing, local teams, market entry for Tanzania/Uganda), (6) Productive use appliance deployment for 50,000 units. Estimate unit economics (cost per connection, revenue per user, payback period) and aggregate to validate the $200-250M total. Phase by year and by country.",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "goal": "Research key risk factors and mitigation mechanisms for off-grid energy financing in East Africa: (1) Currency risk (KES, TZS, UGX depreciation vs USD/EUR-denominated funding—hedging costs, TCX Fund, local currency lending options), (2) Regulatory risk (net metering policies, mini-grid tariff regulations in Kenya/Tanzania/Uganda, import duties on solar equipment), (3) Credit/default risk (PAYGo portfolio performance benchmarks, typical default rates 5-15%, collection mechanisms), (4) Technology risk (battery degradation, equipment warranty structures), (5) Political/country risk (stability assessments, MIGA/ATI coverage options). For each risk, identify specific mitigation instruments and their costs.",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "goal": "Synthesize all research into a comprehensive financing architecture document. Integrate: (1) The capital needs model with phased deployment plan, (2) The layered blended finance structure with specific instrument terms and allocation, (3) The prioritized donor/investor target list matched to each financing layer (who funds what, in what sequence), (4) Risk mitigation mechanisms mapped to each layer, (5) A fundraising sequencing roadmap (what to close first to unlock subsequent tranches—typically grants/TA first, then concessional anchor, then guarantees, then commercial), (6) Key assumptions, sensitivities, and decision points. Present the architecture as a clear waterfall/capital stack diagram description, a 5-year phased fundraising timeline, and a top-20 prioritized investor outreach list with recommended approach sequence. Validate that the structure achieves 4-6% blended returns and can realistically mobilize $200-250M.",
      "tool_name": "reasoning",
      "depends_on": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
    }
  ],
  "dependencies_graph": [
    {"task_idx": "0", "depends_on": []},
